  "content": "Namo guru mañjughoṣāya!You who see and speak,The Knower and the Unsurpassed Teacher,You taught the Victorious One’s dependent arising;I bow to you who see and instruct.Whatever misfortunes exist in the world,Their root is ignorance;You taught that dependent arisingIs what, when seen, reverses that.At that time, how could the wiseNot understand that the path of dependent arisingIs the very coreOf your teaching?This being so, O Protector,What could anyone find as a more wondrous wayTo praise you than [the fact that]You taught dependent arising?Whatever relies on conditions,Is explained as empty of intrinsic nature;What could be more wondrous than thisWay of teaching well?That which, when grasped by the childish,Strengthens the bonds of apprehending extremes,Is for the wise the very gatewayThat severs every web of conceptual elaboration.Since this teaching is not seen elsewhere,You alone are called the Teacher;Just as a lion among jackals,You are supreme even among the non-Buddhists.How wonderful the Teacher! How wonderful the Refuge!How wonderful the Supreme Speaker! How wonderful the Protector!I prostrate to that TeacherWho taught so well dependent arising.You, who act for the benefit of the world,Spoke for the sake of healing;Emptiness, the essence of your teaching,Is a peerless reason for certainty.The mode of dependent arising,Without contradiction and unestablished,By seeing this, how could your systemBe understood in any other way?When you see emptinessAs the meaning of dependent arising,The lack of intrinsic natureAnd the validity of actions do not contradict.But when seen in the opposite way,Actions are not possible in emptiness,And without emptiness, there are no actions;This is considered falling into a perilous abyss.Therefore, in your teaching,Seeing dependent arising is highly praised;That is neither total non-existenceNor existence by way of intrinsic nature.Like a sky-flower, it is independent;Therefore, it does not exist without dependence.If it were established by essence, its arisingWould contradict dependence on causes and conditions.Therefore, since there is no phenomenonThat exists apart from dependent arising,It is taught that there is no phenomenonThat is not empty of intrinsic nature.Because intrinsic nature cannot be reversed,If phenomena possessed any intrinsic nature,Nirvana would not be possible,And it is taught that no conceptual proliferation could be reversed.Therefore, that it is devoid of intrinsic natureIs well proclaimed in the assembly of the wiseAgain and again with a lion’s roar;Who is able to refute this?The two points—that there is no intrinsic nature whatsoever,And that this arises in dependence upon that—Are both valid;Needless to say, they do not contradict each other.By the reason of dependent arising,One does not rely on extreme views;This excellent teaching is the reasonWhy You, O Protector, are the unexcelled speaker.That all these are empty of essence,And that from this cause this effect arises—These two certainties assist one anotherWithout any hindrance.What could be more wonderful than this?What could be more marvelous than this?If one praises You in this manner,It is true praise, and not otherwise.Since they are held as slaves by delusion,Those who harbor resentment toward You—What wonder is there that they cannot bearThe sound of the teaching of no-intrinsic-nature?The cherished treasury of Your speechAccepts dependent arising,Yet they cannot bear the lion’s roar of emptiness;At this, I am filled with wonder.If one grasps at the very nameOf the supreme dependent arising—The gateway to leading others to the nature-less—As being an intrinsic nature, then alas, these people!By what method can one lead themTo that excellent path which You delight in,The peerless gatewayWell-traveled by the supreme noble ones?Intrinsic nature is non-fabricated and independent,While dependent arising is relative and contrived;How can these two, within a single basis,Exist together without contradiction?Therefore, whatever arises in dependence,Though primordially empty of intrinsic nature,Appears as such;Thus, all of this is taught to be like an illusion.That which You have taughtCannot be undermined by any opponentEven in terms of common consensus;This I have understood well.If one asks why, it is because by explaining this,One keeps far away the opportunityFor superimposition and deprecationRegarding things seen and unseen.Seeing that Your speech is peerless,The reason for this being dependent arising,Certainty arises that Your other teachings, too,Are valid authorities.Having seen things as they are, You have spoken well;For those who train in Your footsteps,All decline has become far removed,Because the root of all faults is reversed.By turning away from Your teaching,Even though one may endure long hardship,Faults only increase, as if summoned;Because the view of a self remains firm.How wonderful! When a wise personHas thoroughly understood the difference between these two,Then, from the very marrow of their bones,Why would they not feel devotion to You?What need is there to speak of Your many teachings?Even regarding the meaning of just a fraction of them,Gaining certainty, even if only in a general way,Bestows supreme happiness.Alas! My mind has been conquered by delusion;Though I have long taken refuge in such a mass of excellence,And have long since gone for refuge,I have not understood even a fraction of your qualities.Nevertheless, before the stream of life heading toward the jaws of the Lord of Death has ceased,That I have gained even a slight conviction in you,I consider this, too, to be good fortune.I think of it as a fortunate occasion.Among teachers, the teacher of dependent origination,And among types of wisdom, the knowledge of dependent origination—Like the Sovereign of Victors within the worlds,You alone perfectly understand these supreme excellences, and no one else.Whatever You have commanded,It proceeds by starting from dependent origination itself;That, too, is for the sake of passing beyond suffering;There is nothing in You that does not lead to peace.How wonderful! Your teachings—Whoever they reach the ears of,Because all of them will become peaceful,Who would not feel devotion to upholding Your teachings?Vanquishing all opponents,Free from the contradictions of preceding and following,And bestowing the two benefits upon all living beings—In this system, my joy increases.For the sake of this, You,In some lives gave Your body, and in others Your life,Your beloved kin, and collections of wealth,Repeatedly, for countless eons.By seeing Your qualities,Just as a hook draws a fish,My mind is drawn to that Dharma;Those who have not heard it from You are of little fortune.By the force of that grief,Just as a mother’s mind followsAfter her beloved child,My mind does not let go.When I reflect upon Your words,The Teacher, blazing with the glory of the marks and signs,And encircled by a web of light,With His Brahma-like voice,Thinking, \"Thus He spoke,\"The mere appearance of the Sage’s form in my mindActs as a remedy for those tormentedBy heat, just like moonlight.In this way, that marvelousAnd excellent tradition, by unskilledBeings, like cotton wool,Has been rendered completely confused.Having seen this situation, I,With manifold effort,Followed the wise,And searched for your intention again and again.At that time, when I studiedMany treatises of our own and other schools,My mind was utterly tormentedBy a web of ever-increasing doubts.Your unexcelled vehicle’s way,Having abandoned the extremes of existence and non-existence,Was taught in the commentaries exactly as it is—The kunda-flower grove of Nāgārjuna’s tradition.The mandala of stainless wisdom is full,Moving without obstruction through the sky of the scriptures,Dispelling the heart’s darkness of grasping at extremes,And outshining the constellations of false speakers.Illuminated by the garlands of white lightOf the glorious Chandrakīrti’s elegant explanations,When seen through the kindness of the Guru,My mind attained its rest.Among all deeds, the deed of speechIs supreme; and because this is so,By the wise,Recollect the Buddha through this.Having gone forth in the footsteps of that Teacher,With training in the Victor’s speech that is not inferior,And being a monk diligent in the practice of yoga,I pay homage to that Great Sage in this way.Having encountered the teachings of the unsurpassed Teacher,Such a meeting is due to the kindness of the Guru;Therefore, I dedicate this merit so that all beings without exceptionMay be cared for by a sublime spiritual friend.May the teachings of that Benefactor, until the end of existence,Not be shaken by the winds of false conceptual thoughts,And having known the nature of the teachings, may I always be filledWith the conviction found in the Teacher.May I clarify the reality of dependent arising,And in all my lives, within the excellent tradition of the Sage,Hold it fast, having surrendered even my body and life,Without becoming lax for even a single moment.That supreme Guide, through immeasurable hardships,Accomplished this, having made earnest application the essence;May I pass my days and nights in discernment,Contemplating by what means this may be increased.When I strive in that manner with a pure, superior resolve,May Brahmā, Indra, the World Guardians,And the Excellent Black One and other protectors,Always assist me without distraction.",
